  "marked": false,
  "answer": "<p>The best solution is to deploy two instances of your stateless API in different AWS regions and configure <b>Route53 Latency Based Routing with failover</b> to direct traffic to the optimal region. This method leverages Route53's health checks and failover so clients are automatically routed to the healthy, low-latency region. Using Auto Scaling Groups behind ELBs in both regions guarantees your deployment is elastic and resilient, even during a regional AWS failure.</p><p>The other options are not sufficient for regional failures:</p><ul><li><i>Set up a CloudFront distribution in front of your API. Even if your region encounters downtime, CloudFront's edge infrastructure ensures service continuity.</i> – CloudFront can cache static data and mitigate certain disruptions, but if the API's origin is entirely unavailable due to a regional outage, CloudFront cannot serve dynamic API responses that require a live backend.</li><li><i>Utilize an Elastic Load Balancer (ELB) with cross-zone load balancing for redundancy across data centers, guaranteeing availability if one availability zone is lost.</i> – Cross-zone load balancing helps within a single region but won't protect against complete regional failures, as the ELB itself operates only inside its home region.</li><li><i>Create a Route53 Weighted Round Robin DNS record, so if one region goes down, that region can redirect users to another operational region.</i> – Weighted routing doesn't provide health checks or automated failover, and if a region goes down, that weighted endpoint won’t be able to perform redirects or respond, resulting in downtime for users routed to the failed region.</li></ul><p><b>Key tip:</b> Stateless APIs are easier to deploy across multiple regions for disaster recovery. By pairing multi-region deployments with Route53's advanced routing and health checks, you achieve high resiliency to regional outages.</p>",
  "query": "<p>You need your stateless API to remain operational even if an AWS region becomes unavailable. The API collects data from external sources, doesn't maintain state, and isn't backed by a database. What is an efficient method to ensure high availability during regional outages?</p>",
